B. Correct. A boundary violation involves misuse of the therapeutic relationship. It may exploit the client, impair professional judgment, create pressure or dependency, cause significant harm, or allow the therapist to obtain personal, emotional, financial, romantic, or sexual benefit. The central concern is not simply whether the behavior was unusual. The concern is whether professional power was used in a way that displaced client welfare and damaged the integrity of treatment.
